# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
#
# Unanchored, deliberately: this is a workspace-shaped repository and every
# directory under `packages/` grows its own `node_modules` the moment anyone
# installs there. A leading-slash rule ignores only the root one, which is how
# a dependency tree ends up committed from a subdirectory nobody was watching.
node_modules/
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# package-manager caches and local state
.npm/
.pnpm-store/
.bun/

# testing
coverage/

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# private keys and credential bundles
#
# Filenames, not contents — nothing here scans for a secret, so the list has to
# cover the shapes a key actually arrives in. `client_secret*.json` and
# `service-account*.json` are the exact names Google Cloud hands you when you
# download OAuth or service-account credentials for the Search Console
# integration, and they land in whatever directory the browser was pointed at.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
credentials.json
credentials.*.json
client_secret*.json
service-account*.json
secrets.json
secrets.*.json

# debug / tool logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*
*.log
/logs

# env files
#
# Real values are never committed. The negations below are what make the
# committed *examples* possible: a bare `.env*` (the previous rule) silently
# swallowed `.env.example` too, which is the one env file that MUST be tracked.
# Order matters — the negations have to come after the broad patterns.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# vercel
.vercel

# TinyMCE static assets — copied from node_modules/tinymce by scripts/copy-tinymce.mjs (postinstall)
/public/assets/tinymce

# runtime database — never distribute an instance's data
/data/
*.db
*.db-*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Superpowers brainstorm artifacts
.superpowers/

# Claude Code local tooling (settings, cached skills) — not app source
.claude/

# Cursor local tooling (vendored skills, hooks) — same rationale as .claude/
.cursor/

# Codex local tooling (config.toml) — same rationale as .claude/
.codex/

# Aider / Continue local state — same rationale again
.aider*
.continue/

# Impeccable surface briefs — local design tooling output, not app source
.impeccable/

# JetBrains IDE config
.idea/

# VS Code local workspace state (extensions.json / settings.json may be
# committed deliberately by un-ignoring them if the project ever wants to)
.vscode/

# Private developer documentation (Phase 9.10E). This is the ONE internal
# documentation home for the project: implementation reports, phase reports,
# design specs, plans, release runbooks, decision records and agent working
# knowledge. Nothing under it is ever published. The rules that govern it are
# in dev-docs/AGENT-POLICY.md, which every agent reads before writing a
# document. Anchored to the root: there is exactly one dev-docs.
/dev-docs/

# Agent-facing root documents. Their real, maintained content lives in
# dev-docs/agent/. These two paths are ignored so that a tool which
# auto-discovers or auto-creates one at the root cannot make it public.
/CLAUDE.md
/AGENTS.md

# Local MCP server configuration. It stays on disk because local developer
# tooling reads it from the project root, but it describes this developer's
# machine, not the product. Anchored: only the root copy is expected.
/.mcp.json

# Local secrets scratch file.
#
# Anchored to the root because that is the one that exists, and unanchored as
# well because a copy in a subdirectory is the same problem. Never committed in
# this repository's history — verified before the public release — and it must
# stay that way.
/data-info.txt
data-info.txt

# Package build output and local pack artifacts (Phase 7.2). Generated by
# `npm run build:packages` and `npm pack`; the package.json files that
# describe them ARE tracked.
packages/*/dist
*.tgz


# flowcms:template-strip:start — scaffolder build output, this repository only
# The application template create-flowcms carries (Phase 7.3). Generated from
# this repository by `node scripts/build-create-flowcms.mjs`, along with the
# build stamp beside it. The manifest that describes it — and the CLI itself —
# ARE tracked.
packages/create-flowcms/template
packages/create-flowcms/template.json
# flowcms:template-strip:end
